# Cilium migration on the existing K3s host

Status: preparation and acceptance runbook; no completed migration is claimed.
Audience: the operator responsible for the host, cluster and existing applications.

The [roadmap](../ROADMAP.md#near-term-sequence) places this maintenance after
Argo adoption, required worker readiness, issue remediation, central configuration
and real pipeline verification. This document does not change that order.

## Scope and prerequisites

`scripts/deploy-cilium.sh install` implements a disruptive, single-node,
same-Pod-CIDR cutover. It does not prepare the host, stop workloads, recycle old
sandboxes, restore host configuration or prove application connectivity. It keeps
the node cordoned. Do not use it for a multi-node or different-CIDR migration.

The selected values in `my-values/infra/cilium-values.yaml` retain kube-proxy,
use VXLAN and cluster-pool IPv4 IPAM. Compare their actual CIDR and mask with
the existing cluster before scheduling work. These are selected deployment
values, not universal K3s defaults. The script selects the chart version explicitly;
preserve that exact archive and its images for installation and recovery.

Upstream documents disabling Flannel and the K3s network-policy controller when
using another CNI. Disabling the controller does not remove its existing rules.
Inspect effective service arguments as well as configuration files; saving one
YAML file is not sufficient to reconstruct the host. See
[K3s networking options](https://docs.k3s.io/networking/basic-network-options) and
[network-policy cleanup](https://docs.k3s.io/networking/networking-services).
The upstream [node-by-node migration](https://docs.cilium.io/en/stable/installation/k8s-install-migration/)
uses a different procedure; do not combine its allocator/policy settings with
this repository's single-node same-CIDR procedure.

Before changing anything, record a private maintenance manifest containing:

- Source revision, chart archive checksum, image digests and effective K3s,
  containerd, Kubernetes, kernel and CNI versions.
- Node identity, Pod/Service CIDRs, routes, interfaces, CNI configuration,
  existing policy rules and the effective service/configuration files needed
  to return to the previous state. Keep credentials out of shared logs.
- Every namespace, workload owner, DaemonSet, Pod network mode, storage dependency,
  required network edge and application owner. Resolve Paperless/HomeOS from
  actual deployment; do not assume the literal `paperless` namespace is correct.
- Verified independent host/KVM access, offline instructions, reachable local
  recovery artifacts, tested data backups, maintenance window and recovery deadline.
- Explicit workload stop/restart order, how each unmanaged Pod is recreated,
  and a host-specific rollback procedure tested on an isolated equivalent host.

An absent inventory, untested independent access or unspecified rollback is a
stop condition. An exported kubeconfig that requires the failed CNI is not an
independent recovery path. Do not infer approval for data loss from a maintenance
window. Use the [AX41 host contract](../operations/ax41-rollout.md) only when it
matches the actual selected host.

## Host preflight and controlled installation

1. Quiesce writes and stop affected workloads according to the approved inventory.
   Preserve databases, PVCs and Secrets. Cordon the selected node and establish
   the independent host session before interrupting networking.
2. Apply the reviewed host configuration that disables the old CNI/controller
   while retaining the chosen API, service and kube-proxy configuration. Reconcile
   command-line overrides; use the host-specific restart/recovery procedure.
3. Inspect the real CRI sandboxes. All old ordinary ready sandboxes must be gone
   before the new allocator runs. Cordoning alone does not stop existing Pods or
   DaemonSets. Unknown namespace mode is a failure, not an exemption.
4. Confirm API access, preserved data, correct CIDRs and offline recovery ability.
   Set `CILIUM_K3S_READY=true` only after recording these actual preflight results.
   The variable is an operator assertion; the script cannot verify the entire host.
5. Run `scripts/deploy-cilium.sh install` from the reviewed checkout on that host.
   The script rechecks the flag, single cordoned node and ready CRI sandbox modes,
   installs the selected chart and applies the cluster policy file. Failure keeps
   the node cordoned; it does not roll back host state automatically.

Do not copy a generic install or uninstall pipeline onto an existing host.
Do not use a blanket iptables flush, remove all CNI state or delete arbitrary
interfaces as a recovery shortcut. The maintenance manifest must name the exact
state owned by each CNI and the verified phase-specific inverse operations.

## Policies and workload restart

`my-values/infra/cilium-cluster-policies.yaml` supplies the global baseline and
DNS allowance. Its bootstrap namespace exclusions are explicit exceptions;
excluded namespaces are not proven protected by the ordinary-workload baseline.
The existing temporary Paperless exception requires a matching actual owner and
namespace. No additional project exception is implied.

Workload owners supply namespaced policies for their actual dependencies.
Review API, DNS, model gateway, registry, Redis, databases, SPIRE, Tailscale and
management routes before starting dependents. Hubble Relay/UI access must retain
their selected private-access policy. Neither green agents nor a policy object's
existence proves enforcement.

Use `scripts/migrate-kubeclaw-network-policies-to-cilium.sh` only after inspecting
its selected namespace and policy files. Its verification calls
`scripts/verify-cilium-policies.py`, which compares source policy specs with live
specs and checks reported Cilium endpoint state/revision. That verifier does not
establish that every workload is managed or that a forbidden connection is denied.

Before uncordoning, verify policy admission, installed specs and agent realization.
Then deliberately resume scheduling and recreate inventoried workloads in their
dependency order. Hubble Relay/UI readiness can require this scheduling step.
Record each ordinary Pod UID and its matching Cilium endpoint; account explicitly
for host-network Pods and reject missing, stale or unmanaged ordinary Pods.

## Acceptance and evidence

Bind results to the maintenance manifest, actual Pod/endpoint identities and
source/configuration digests. The mandatory cases are:

| Case | Required observation |
| --- | --- |
| Inventory coverage | Every current ordinary Pod is managed by the intended Cilium agent; no old allocator remains active. |
| Policy realization | Installed specs match source, endpoint revisions are realized and no endpoint is in an error state. |
| Positive connections | Actual application operations succeed across each inventoried required dependency. |
| Negative connections | A working probe reaches the intended boundary and a forbidden peer/port is denied; a broken probe or absent server does not count. |
| Pipeline | A real project completes its required delivery flow; failure and cleanup paths still behave correctly. |
| Existing applications | Owners run their actual application operations; if Paperless is deployed, include upload, OCR and retrieval. |
| Cleanup | Targeted removal of proven legacy policy/state does not change intended connectivity; repeat positive and negative cases afterward. |
| Restart | Planned Pod/agent restarts retain connectivity, policy enforcement and data. |
| Recovery | Independent host access works and the rehearsed rollback restores the former network and application operations. |

Only after these results and legacy cleanup pass may the operator create the
`kubeclaw-cutover-verified` ConfigMap in namespace `cilium`, attaching the exact
evidence identity. The current upgrade script checks marker existence only;
existence is not an independent verification of those results. Do not create a
marker to bypass incomplete first-install acceptance.

## Failure and rollback boundaries

Before allocator activation, restore the exact saved effective host settings and
verify the original CNI before restarting applications. After any Cilium-assigned
Pod exists, prevent concurrent allocation first: quiesce affected workloads,
inventory/remove their network sandboxes through the host's reviewed procedure,
then restore the old allocator and recreate workloads. Do not leave old and new
same-CIDR allocators active together.

After the acceptance marker exists, a regression still invalidates the affected
operational claim. Record the failing identities and suspend upgrade use of that
marker until resolved. Do not delete PVCs, Secrets or SPIRE state as a networking
repair. If the exact rollback cannot be established, keep dependent workloads
stopped and use independent host recovery; do not uncordon merely to see whether
the cluster recovers.

The host-specific maintenance manifest and real acceptance results are still
required. This runbook alone does not close IFR-02-001 or claim a rehearsed rollback.
